      "body": "## Motivation\n\n[nvidia/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-NVFP4](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-NVFP4) stores the two Engram lookup tables (`layers.{1,14}.engram.embed.weight`) as packed MXFP4: `uint8 [rows, 128]`, two E2M1 values per byte with the even column in the low nibble, next to the unchanged per-32 ue8m0 `.embed.scale`. SGLang allocates the table as e4m3 `[rows, 256]`, so `EngramEmbedding._load_rows` fails on the shape mismatch and the checkpoint cannot load. Everything else in the checkpoint already loads: the NVFP4 routed experts take the existing hybrid FP8 + NVFP4 path, and the DSpark draft keeps its MXFP4 experts. Keeping the table packed and decoding in the gather (as vllm-project/vllm#61530 does) would halve the table memory; that is a larger follow-up.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- `EngramEmbedding._load_rows`: when the loaded rows are `uint8` and the table is e4m3, widen this rank's rows into the e4m3 table instead of copying them. The widening runs in 1M-row chunks on the table's device and handles both the per-rank and the shared (row-slice) placements.\n- Every E2M1 value (0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 6 and their negatives) is exact in e4m3 and the scale tensor is unchanged, so the dequantized table is identical to an MXFP4 decode. The lookup, staging, host-table and peer-shard paths are unchanged, and the table keeps the FP8 checkpoint's memory.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nGB300, TP4, one node, SGLang nightly 2026-10-30 plus this change, GSM8K via sgl-eval 0.1.3 (300 questions, temperature 1, top_p 1). Both checkpoints ran back to back on the same node.\n\n| checkpoint | plain | DSpark (block 5) | DSpark accept length |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| nvidia/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-NVFP4 | 98.00% | 98.33% | 3.48 |\n| deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (FP8) | 98.00% | 97.33% | 3.57 |\n\nThe logs show the hybrid FP8 + NVFP4 checkpoint detection and the `flashinfer_trtllm_routed` MoE runner for the NVFP4 checkpoint. GPU memory after load is 77.3 GB per GPU vs 99.9 GB for the FP8 checkpoint.\n\nLoad-path checks (CPU): the widened table matches a reference `decode_e2m1 * 2^e8m0` decode bit for bit, and its fp8 bytes equal torch's fp32 -> e4m3 cast. Cases: all 256 byte values with random scales, 100003 random rows across chunk boundaries, an empty shard, a row-slice destination, and 8 real 4096-row ranges of both tables. Swapping the nibble order fails the check, which confirms the low-nibble-first layout.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nLoad only. Widening runs at about 3.8 GB/s on 16 CPU threads, about 6 s per TP4 rank shard of a table when the table lives in host memory.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\n- [ ] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests). No new unit test: the load path needs the real checkpoint; the bit-exactness checks are listed above.\n- [x] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations). No user-facing option changes.\n- [x] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n## Review and Merge Process\n\n1. Ping Merge Oncalls to start the process. See the [PR Merge Process](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.github/MAINTAINER.md#pull-request-merge-process).\n2. Get approvals from [CODEOWNERS](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.github/CODEOWNERS) and other reviewers.\n3. Trigger CI tests with [comments](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#how-to-trigger-ci-tests) or contact authorized users to do so.\n   - Common commands include `/tag-and-rerun-ci`, `/tag-run-ci-label`, `/rerun-failed-ci`\n4. After green CI and required approvals, ask Merge Oncalls or people with Write permission to merge the PR.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #41412214970](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41412214970)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #41412214971](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41412214971)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #41412215152](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41412215152)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Base CI only covers the core cases, and its workload may not exercise your change. Use `/rerun-test` to run the tests your change touches, or copy this prompt for an agent:\n>\n> ```text\n> Find the registered tests this PR affects and run them with /rerun-test, following https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.agents/skills/rerun-test/SKILL.md\n> ```\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
